workedExamples.solution_text:
Take out the common factor $x$, then factorise the difference of squares:
$\begin{aligned}y&=x^3-36x\\&=x(x^2-36)\\&=x(x-6)(x+6)\end{aligned}$

The leading term is $x^3$, so the left end falls and the right end rises.

Let $x=0$: $y=0$.

Let $y=0$:
$0=x(x-6)(x+6)$.
By the null factor law, the zeroes are $0,6,-6$. The common factor $x$ gives the zero at the origin. All three zeroes are distinct, so the curve crosses at each intercept.

